To determine the curve of quickest descent, we first investigate how the velocity of a particle changes as it moves under the influence of gravity.
Consider a particle that starts from rest and slides along a smooth curve without friction. Since no non-conservative forces are present, the total mechanical energy remains constant throughout the motion. Let \(y\) denote the vertical distance descended from the starting point. The loss of gravitational potential energy is converted entirely into kinetic energy, giving
\[
mgy = \frac{1}{2}mv^2
\]
After cancelling the mass \(m\), we obtain
\[
v = \sqrt{2gy}
\]
where \(g\) is the acceleration due to gravity and \(v\) is the speed of the particle.
This equation shows that the speed of the particle depends only on its vertical position. As the particle descends, its speed increases. Therefore, the total travel time depends not only on the length of the path but also on how quickly the particle gains speed along that path.
Consequently, the shortest path is not necessarily the fastest path.

3.3 From Classical Optimization to the Calculus of Variations
In elementary calculus, optimization problems involve finding the maximum or minimum value of a function.
For example, given
\[
f(x) = x^2 - 4x + 3
\]
the optimal value can be found by solving
\[
f'(x) = 0
\]
The brachistochrone problem is fundamentally different.
Instead of selecting a single value of \(x\), we must determine an entire function \(y(x)\). Every possible curve connecting the two endpoints produces a different value of the travel-time integral
\[
T = \int_{x_1}^{x_2} \frac{\sqrt{1 + y'^2}}{\sqrt{2gy}} \, dx
\]
The mathematical discipline developed to solve such problems is known as the calculus of variations.
Rather than optimizing numbers, the calculus of variations optimizes functions. This idea represented a major breakthrough in mathematics and became the foundation for many later developments in physics and engineering.
